course: dictionary
lesson: 229
title: Allergy: when the body overreacts
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An allergy is when your body reacts badly to something that is harmless for most people. Pollen, nuts, cats and bee stings can all cause allergies. The body is not being silly. It is just reacting far too strongly.
explain: «Itchy red eyes every spring are usually pollen allergy, which you cannot catch from anyone.» The clue is every spring: the eyes itch at the same time each year, when pollen is in the air. You cannot catch an allergy like a cold, because it comes from how your own body reacts.
explain: «Hay fever is an allergy to pollen.» Hay fever is the common name for this kind of allergy. The reaction can be sneezing, itching, a rash or a runny nose. Sometimes it is serious, and trouble breathing needs emergency help straight away.
fact: An allergy is when the body reacts badly to something harmless for most people.
fact: Common allergies include nuts, pollen, cats and bee stings.
fact: An allergic reaction can be sneezing, itching, a rash, swelling or serious trouble breathing.
fact: «Allergic» is the adjective: «Sam is allergic to cats.»
fact: Hay fever is an allergy to pollen, and it is often worse on dry, windy days.
fact: Schools often have nut-free rules, because a nut allergy can be dangerous.
exercise: «Itchy red eyes every spring are usually pollen allergy.» What is causing the itchy eyes? | Too much sunshine | A dirty bedroom | *Pollen in the air | A cold you caught from a friend
exercise: What is an allergy? | A sudden loud noise | *When your body reacts badly to something harmless for most people | Catching a germ from someone else | Feeling tired after a long day
exercise: Find the adjective in «Sam is allergic to cats.» | Sam | cats | is | *allergic
test: What could happen if you share food with someone who has an allergy? | *Even a tiny amount could cause a dangerous reaction | It will taste better | Nothing, because a tiny amount is always safe | They will feel happier
test: When is hay fever usually worse, according to the text? | In the middle of a snowy winter | When you are asleep | On quiet, wet days | *On dry, windy days
exam: Which of these is a typical allergic reaction? | *A runny nose | A long walk home | Doing a sum in your head | A bright sunny sky
